\textbf{Why RevIN destroys routing: formal analysis.} Each input window $\mathbf{x}_i$ decomposes into mean $M_i = \mu(\mathbf{x}_i)$, scale $\Sigma_i = \sigma(\mathbf{x}_i)$, and shape $\mathbf{S}_i = (\mathbf{x}_i - M_i)/\Sigma_i$. RevIN maps $\mathbf{x}_i \mapsto \mathbf{S}_i$, discarding $(M_i, \Sigma_i)$.

\begin{proposition}[Routing Information Loss Under RevIN]
\label{thm:mi_decomp}
Let $X$ be a random input window, $E \in \{1,\ldots,K\}$ the expert assignment produced by any deterministic router, and $S = (X - M)/\Sigma$ the RevIN-normalized shape.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\textup{(i)}] \textbf{Exact decomposition.} $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = I(M, \Sigma; E \mid S) \geq 0$.
    \item[\textup{(ii)}] \textbf{Unconditional quantitative bound.} Without any independence assumption,
    \[
        I(X; E) - I(S; E) \;\geq\; I(M, \Sigma;\, E) \;-\; I(M, \Sigma;\, S),
    \]
    where the residual $\varepsilon := I(M, \Sigma;\, S) \geq 0$ measures the ``leakage'' of stripped statistics through the shape.  Equality (tightness) holds iff $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ given $E$.
    \item[\textup{(iii)}] \textbf{Full-information corner.} If the router depends \emph{entirely} on the stripped statistics ($E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M,\Sigma)$), then $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = H(E)$: RevIN destroys the \emph{entire} routing distribution in nats.
    \item[\textup{(iv)}] \textbf{Empirical leakage and signal ratio.} The leakage $\varepsilon$ varies by dataset ($0.09$--$1.26$ nats via CCA; cross-validated within ${\pm}0.2$ nats by the distribution-free KSG estimator~\citep{kraskov2004ksg}). The bound~(ii) is informative when $I(M,\Sigma; E) \gg \varepsilon$. The signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M) + \mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ serves as a monotone proxy for $I(M,\Sigma; E)$: high-$R$ datasets have large routing information relative to $\varepsilon$, producing large raw-routing gains; low-$R$ datasets have $I(M,\Sigma; E) \approx \varepsilon$, producing no gain---validated at $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ across nine datasets.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Full proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}. \textit{Remark.} $R$ is monotone in $I(M,\Sigma; E)$: higher location-scale variance gives the softmax router more discriminative power; dividing by $\overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ accounts for leakage $\varepsilon$. KSG cross-check (${\pm}0.26$ nats) confirms this holds beyond Gaussianity.

\textbf{Quantitative prediction.} The \emph{routing signal ratio} $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\text{Var}(M) + \text{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\text{Var}(S)}$ serves as a monotone proxy for the MI bound. Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio} validates this across nine LTSF benchmarks: $\rho = -0.88$ ($p < 0.002$; bootstrap CI: $[-1.00, -0.51]$; $n{=}9$). Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) is correctly predicted as a boundary case ($\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$). Solar ($R{=}0.06$) is the one outlier: the Conv1d router exploits temporal shape patterns beyond location-scale, consistent with the SSR ablation ($+43\%$ degradation). Additional ablations (SSR, RDGF, per-quartile analysis) in Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}.

