\textbf{Cross-backbone generalization.}\label{sec:cross_backbone} MOMENT-small/large (RevIN): \textbf{all 18} win ($27$--$81\%$). Moirai (LayerNorm): \textbf{18/18} ($3$--$31\%$). Moirai-MoE~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe} (native sparse MoE): \textbf{all 6 datasets} ($-37\%$ to $-90\%$; Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}), confirming adapter-level routing stacks with backbone-internal routing. \textbf{Negative controls}---Chronos and Timer-XL~\citep{liu2025timerxl} (no instance norm): no collapse (entropy $1.48$--$1.61$), RR-MoA provides no advantage (\textbf{4/18}, within chance)---exactly as Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}).

\textbf{Sparse routing, multi-horizon, and task generalization.}\label{sec:topk}
Top-2 adds $4.3$\,ms ($+10\%$); $K{\in}\{3,5,7,10\}$ changes MSE ${\leq}2.7\%$. RR-MoA wins \textbf{24/24} horizons ($H{\in}\{96{-}720\}$), generalizes to imputation ($7/8$ datasets, $-44\%$ to $-77\%$) and classification ($86.5\%$ on BasicMotions; Appendix~\ref{app:topk},~\ref{app:imputation}).

\section{Conclusion}

{\looseness=-1 We identified \emph{normalization-induced co-adaptation} as the cause of MoE routing collapse on TSFMs, proved it via opposite entropy trajectories (Figure~\ref{fig:causal_contrast}), and validated the theory at $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($n{=}9$). \textbf{RR-MoA} breaks the collapse (\textbf{54/54 wins}, Frozen Paradox $12$--$79\%$ over full FT); \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} matches DLinear on \textbf{108/123 cells} across six backbones (Timer-XL $12/12$; Moirai-MoE $15/15$). \textbf{Practitioner guideline:} compute $R(\mathcal{D})$ before deploying MoE adapters; $R{>}0.5$ predicts raw routing helps, $R{<}0.2$ predicts it does not. \textbf{Limitations:} ETTh1 $+2.2\%$ gap; Time-MoE untested; Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} linear only.}

