All three normalizers cause entropy collapse to ${\leq}0.004$, while removing normalization entirely keeps entropy stable at $0.817$. The mechanism is the same in each case: the normalizer strips per-window statistics before the encoder, the router receives homogenized hidden states, and gradient co-adaptation amplifies the initial routing bias into full collapse. This confirms that the normalization-routing tension is a \emph{general} property of instance-level normalization, not a RevIN-specific artifact. Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts that collapse occurs specifically when the stripped statistics carry routing signal; a cross-modality control (Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}) validates this prediction.

\subsection{Cross-Modality Control: Vision MoE}
\label{app:vision_moe}

To test whether normalization-induced routing collapse transfers beyond time series, we construct a controlled vision experiment: ResNet-18 (pretrained on ImageNet, adapted for CIFAR-10) as backbone, with $K{=}5$ architecturally diverse expert classifiers (mean-pool, max-pool, attention-pool, conv-pool, last-patch---matching the time-series expert pool), no load-balancing loss, and the same four conditions tested in the time-series experiments.

